   "subject": "Re: [simmer-devel] rollback to top of branch",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "2018-04-26 16:47 GMT+02:00 Paul &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">guey...@gmail.com</a>&gt;:\r<br>&gt;&gt; This trajectory allows a patient to be sent for tests more than once,\r<br>&gt;&gt; because you are exposing them to the branch decision more than once.\r<br>&gt;&gt; Is that what you want?\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Ah, i think i misunderstood rollback. I don&#39;t want patients to be sent for\r<br>&gt; tests more than once. I thought that rollback (times=1) would stop that.\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "The &quot;times&quot; argument (infinite by default) defines the number of times\r<br>that a particular arrival would rollback upon reaching this activity.\r<br>It&#39;s equivalent to a do/for loop where &quot;times&quot; is the counter.\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; I want some proportion to go for tests, the other proportion to be admitted\r<br>&gt; or discharged.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; The people that go for tests are then admitted or discharged.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; I&#39;m guessing rollback isn&#39;t the option here? Would the solution be to do two\r<br>&gt; branches, one to do the tests (yes/no) and then all of them go to a second\r<br>&gt; branch admitted/discharged?\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Yes, a second branch is the solution. But I would define each\r<br>subtrajectory separatedly:\r<br>\r<br>test &lt;- trajectory() %&gt;%\r<br>...\r<br>admit &lt;- trajectory() %&gt;%\r<br>...\r<br>discharge &lt;- trajectory() %&gt;%\r<br>...\r<br>\r<br>and then,\r<br>\r<br>patient &lt;- trajectory() %&gt;%\r<br>  branch(\r<br>    option=..., continue=...,\r<br>    admit,\r<br>    discharge,\r<br>    test %&gt;%\r<br>      branch(\r<br>        option=..., continue=...,\r<br>        admit,\r<br>        discharge\r<br>      )\r<br>  )\r<br>\r<br>Iñaki\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
